detail.abstract:
Asia faces a distinctive structural paradox: the very institutional arrangements that generated three decades of unprecedented prosperity-export-led growth, supplychain integration, capital-intensive modernization-now prevent the transformative restructuring climate severity demands.&nbsp; 
<div>
 <i>This paper empirically examines this paradox through analysis of transformation implementation</i> in 15 Asian economies (2024-2025), revealing that <i>export concentration creates asymmetric lock-in preventing voluntary transformation.</i>&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 We present three core arguments departing from mainstream adaptation discourse.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 First, Asia's export-dependent model functions as institutional "lock-out," making transformation politically impossible for governments dependent on export revenue-a mechanism absent in developed economies. Second, transformation costs distribute catastrophically unequally, with bottom 40% bearing 6-8x higher costs than top 20%, enabling elite capture that perpetuates inequality while pursuing sustainability rhetoric. Third, transformative resilience in Asia requires inversion of global priorities: mitigation (preventing warming) must precede adaptation (living with warming), because transformation faces hard physical limits in waterscarce, heat-prone geographies where adaptation becomes impossible.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 We conclude that Asia's transformation cannot proceed through market-friendly pathways or incremental institutional reform. Instead, fundamental restructuring requires paradigm revision toward: (1) mitigation-as-prerequisite rather than mitigation-as-optional; (2) justice-centered rather than growth-centered frameworks; (3) regional value-chain integration breaking export concentration; (4) managed contraction alongside transformation, acknowledging some sectors/regions must decline, not merely shift.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Strategic recommendations target fiscal policy redesign (progressive taxation, carbon pricing, formal economy expansion), regional integration architecture (RCEP+, preferential green trade), and sectoral transition financing ($50-100B annually mechanism design) specifically calibrated to Asia's institutional realities.
</div>
